| Who implements and who calls each gRPC method across languages? | `tools/grpcjoin.lua [<corpus>|<dir>]` |\n\n## Templates, clones, the algebra\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| Other implementations of one concept, incomplete ones included | `tools/variants.lua <corpus|dir> --query <name>` |\n| What should a template be able to say? What do container members share? | `tools/divergecensus.lua <corpus|path>`, `tools/membercensus.lua <corpus|dir>` |\n| The helper signature of every near pair; does the pairwise lgg compose? | `tools/hocensus.lua`, `tools/familydiff.lua <corpus|dir>` |\n| Which syntactic positions does the minting rule not cover? | `tools/mintcensus.lua <corpus|dir> [--lang L]` |\n| Drive the vendored algebra with real IR; how much of it is absorbed; has the vendored copy drifted? | `tools/algebradrive.lua`, `tools/elemdrive.lua`, `tools/algebraledger.lua`, `tools/vendordrift.lua [--gate]` |\n\n## Performance\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| Every shipped performance lens over every function, with its denominator | `tools/perfscan.lua <root> [--files <pat>]` |\n| Redundant idempotent setup, and where one copy would do | `tools/redundancy.lua <root>` |\n| What a file's references resolve to (fact cost); profile-guided index-a-scan | `tools/factcost.lua <file.lua>…`, `tools/idxprofile.lua <module> <file.lua> <workload.lua>` |\n| Does `pattern_degree` agree with Lua's own matcher? | `tools/patternjoin.lua ['<pattern>' ...]` |\n| The runtime tier from outside the process (OpenTelemetry spans) | `tools/otelobserve.lua <corpus|dir> --spans <file>` |\n| What BREAKS if this Lua is transliterated to JavaScript? (the census before any emitter) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/jsbreaks.lua <dir> [--show CLASS|key]` — tree-sitter QUERIES over every .lua file (declarative, every byte covered), each site in a break class by whether an ALWAYS-FAITHFUL template exists (faithful for every runtime type, never \"if the inferred type is right\"): TEMPLATE / PARTIAL (a derivable subset: RegExp-translatable patterns, the `continue` goto) / NONE (load, setfenv, %b/%f, general goto, collectgarbage) / DECISION (depends on a REPRESENTATION choice: strings as bytes or UTF-16, tables as Map / object / array) / HOST (vim.*, io, os, ffi — a declared template per member, or a refusal). Vocabularies come from the running Lua (`string`, `_G`). Controls: calls, definitions, labels. MEASURED 2026-09-29 over lua/ (319 files, 7.6 MB): NONE 77 · DECISION 32665 (t[expr] 16353, # 8131, t[number] 2252, byte-offset string methods 2744, non-ASCII string literals 1678 in 152 files) · PARTIAL 8729 · HOST 1923 (65 roots, 120+ vim.x.y members) · TEMPLATE 47896; files free of NONE/DECISION/HOST: 4 of 319. |\n| TRANSLITERATE a Lua tree to JavaScript and see what breaks (emit, parse, load) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/luajs.lua <lua dir> <out dir> [--run]` — `cartograph.luajs` emits every file as a CommonJS module expanded into the template pack `lua/cartograph/luajs/pack.js` (faithful for every runtime type: truthiness, and/or, arithmetic with string coercion, floored %, %.14g concatenation, byte strings, multiple values, 1-based arrays; tables by SHAPE from `cartograph.tblshape` — ARRAY `$arr` / RECORD `$rec` / MAP `$map`, accesses polymorphic `$idx`/`$set`; a Lua local gets a fresh JS name whenever the name is visible). Three layers: EMIT refusals by name (a general goto, a non-continue label, a multi-valued last entry of a Map constructor), PARSE by node's own parser (`node --check`, must be 0), RUN each module loaded under node, by outcome (ok / LuaBreak = a pack gap reached, named / LuaError / JS error). MEASURED 2026-09-29 over lua/ (321 files, 36 s): 26 refusals in 9 files, 0 parse failures; modules that LOAD: 254 → 266 (metatables: a WeakMap by identity, 5.1 semantics) → 267 (HOST pack: io files as userdata, os dates/env/exit/remove/rename/execute, `debug.getinfo(1, \"S\")` mapped back to the Lua source via LUAJS_SRC_ROOT); → 273 (Lua patterns: LuaJIT's matcher transliterated from C by tools/cjs.lua) → 314 of 326 (the VIM host pack: nvim's OWN pure-Lua runtime — vim/_core/shared, inspect, uri, fs, the module set DERIVED from the pack's requires — transliterated beside the modules and loaded lazily as nvim defers it; vim.uv / fn / json / mpack / system / env / NIL / log / notify as declared templates matched to probed nvim behaviour; the editor surfaces abort by name at the read; a shape is an optimization — a key the shape cannot hold goes to a hidden SIDE Map, never an abort; pcall never swallows a LuaBreak). The rest, every one a named break: loadstring 7 (Lua source built at run time), the editor 4, a script without its job file 1. The SEMANTICS oracle is `tests/luajs_spec.lua` (differential: Lua with the standard print vs the emitted JS under node, byte-identical). CART-1197 |\n| Declare HOW a Lua construct becomes JS as a PAIR OF EXAMPLES (not code) — the local half of the transliterator | `lua/cartograph/luajs/rules.lua` `M.RULES`: `{ lua = 'a + b', js = '$add(a, b)' }` — both sides READ by the lossless reader (`cartograph.algebraread`, the JS side as javascript), every Lua identifier a hole, the left MATCHED on a node's term modulo layout (gaps and comments dropped), the right INSTANTIATED and printed (algebra match / instantiate / cst_print); hole modes `as = { f = 'name' \\| 'raw' \\| 'block' }`; compile refuses a rule that drops a hole. 31 rules (literals, grouping, indexing, the operators, do, return 0/1); the CONTEXT cases (scope, calls, tables, loops, if, goto, number/string values) stay in `cartograph.luajs`. `algebraread.read_tree(src, lang, tree, visit)` gives each node of a caller's tree its term. `tools/luajs.lua` reports `RULES: n declared, m match(es); k never fired` (a rule no source reaches has no witness). Acceptance of the move: all 357 emitted files BYTE-IDENTICAL to the hand emitter; luajs_spec pins every rule firing — which then surfaced CART-1200 (no values returned ONE nil), fixed as a deliberate output change (`return $mv();`, normalized diff otherwise empty). CART-1199 |\n| Check the Lua→JS transliteration on EVERY program up to a size (not a sample) — control flow, value counts, coercions | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/luajsgen.lua <control\\|values\\|coercion> <max size> [corpus dir] [out dir] [min=<n>]` — `cartograph.luajs.gencheck`: every program of the fragment runs under real Lua (luaref) and, transliterated, under node, compared per program. Prints the COUNT per size first, then the FUNNEL (generated → parses → loads → terminates → emitted → compared → diverged, + Lua-rejected programs the emitter translated anyway), a census of the compared programs by token, UNREACHED tokens (the claim is vacuous for them), divergences by class (value · message · crash · hang, smallest first; a failed node batch is bisected to its program), and the CLAIM with its four parameters (\"exhaustive to size N over the productions of CORPUS ∩ fragment F, atoms P\" — never promoted to proven by raising N). MEASURED 2026-09-29 at size 8 over lua/ (every divergence in <out>/diverged.txt): control 114,838 compared / 0 diverged (goto in 758, for in 512; atoms incl. 0), values 53,815 / 0 value divergences (72 message-only: variable names, CART-1207), coercion 352,161 / 20 (all a non-integer pow: V8 vs glibc, one ulp, CART-1208). FIXED along the way, each pinned in luajs_spec: the numeric-for check ($forprep), `return ...` (all the varargs), %.14g exponent threshold + exact ties to even + -0, the compare message, LuaJIT hex floats; probing its gaps: a zero for-step counts UP (the sign bit). Lua-rejected programs the emitter translates: CART-1209. CART-1206 |\n| Derive a GRAMMAR from a corpus and enumerate EVERY tree up to a size (any tree-sitter language) | `require('cartograph.grammargen')`: `derive({ {name, text} }, lang)` reads each file with the lossless reader and records every node's SKELETON (tokens + slots; a one-text leaf is