server-root>] --rows` |\n| What each handler SENDS, as a term (complete / partial / one-of several records / opaque) and whether it is on the wire — through case/if joins, patterns, and the callees' clauses (tree + dependency + OTP source) | `tools/xmppserver.lua --sends [--rows] [--otp <OTP src root>]` (default: `~/git/otp_src_<installed version>`) (ejabberd: 124 of 184 sites name one record, was 80; 22 more one of several; all on the wire) |\n| What does the server REPLY to each client request — the handler run with the client's own request term (one of several replies when its arms differ), and how many of the reply's unknowns the request fills | `tools/xmppmerge.lua --responses [--rows] [--otp DIR]`; library `xmppserver.responses(merged, dir, {E, otp})` (CART-1135) |\n| Does the spec-driven ENCODER (record -> XML, `xmppspec.encode`) agree with xmpp's own generated encoder, entry by entry? | `tools/xmppencode.lua [--rows] [--only NAME]` (evaluates each entry's `encode_<name>` from source; CART-1138) |\n| The XMPP WIRE as an algebra grammar (bytes <-> #xmlel <-> record) — do its laws hold, and does it agree with the running fxml byte for byte? | `tools/xmppgrammar.lua [--rows]`; library `xmppgrammar.new{spec, otp}` / `.register(G)` (A.grammar 'xmpp': an embed prints and parses through it), `.read`, `.print_el` (CART-1138) |\n| What does a NIF return (jid's splitter, stringprep) — can a JID the tree reads be known, and do the models agree with the running runtime? | `tools/p1natives.lua [--len N] [--rows]` (exhaustive over the bytes that matter); library `p1natives.models`, handed to `erlterms.program{natives}` (on by default in `xmppserver.program`) — consulted only where the source is absent or a nif_error stub (CART-1138) |\n| Generate a CLIENT of a server from its implementation (every request its handlers accept, every reply they can give) — and check it: contract, standalone, in-process imitation, coverage of the real client | `tools/peergen.lua [--out FILE] [--rows] [--merge] [--twice]` (XMPP IQ), `tools/peergen.lua --grpc ROOT [--client DIR]` (gRPC from .proto: vendored copies merged, drift kept); core `peergen.generate(model)` (protocol-free), adapters `xmpppeer.model(root, {spec, otp})`, `grpcpeer.model(root)` (CART-1138) |\n| Which clause does each erlang call reach — and which clauses does NO call reach (dead clauses), or which calls does no clause take (a function_clause waiting)? | `tools/erlclauses.lua [<root>] [--rows]`; library `erlclauses.census(dir, {E})` (CART-1110) |\n| What the client BUILDS: every `stx` template as an element tree with holes and namespaces | `tools/stxcensus.lua <dir> [--all] [--stanzas] [--json <out>]` |\n| What `xmpp_codec.spec` declares, and its consistency oracles (records, OTP's own parser) | `tools/xmppspeccensus.lua [--rows] [--erl]` |\n| Every record reference against its module's scope, with dependency roots; the compiler as oracle | `tools/erlrecordcensus.lua [--root DIR] [--app NAME=DIR] [--lib DIR] [--rows] [--erl]` |\n| Registered vs declared namespaces, joined by URI (the older CONFORM) | `tools/rootjoin.lua [<left>] [<right>] [--rows]` |\n| Could we emit a client for this endpoint, and if not, who blocks it? | `tools/endpointcensus.lua <corpus|dir> [--rows]` |\n| Who implements and who calls each gRPC method across languages? | `tools/grpcjoin.lua [<corpus>|<dir>]` |\n\n## Templates, clones, the algebra\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| Other implementations of one concept, incomplete ones included | `tools/variants.lua <corpus|dir> --query <name>` |\n| What should a template be able to say? What do container members share? | `tools/divergecensus.lua <corpus|path>`, `tools/membercensus.lua <corpus|dir>` |\n| The helper signature of every near pair; does the pairwise lgg compose? | `tools/hocensus.lua`, `tools/familydiff.lua <corpus|dir>` |\n| Which syntactic positions does the minting rule not cover? | `tools/mintcensus.lua <corpus|dir> [--lang L]` |\n| Drive the