ember | class | all | clean). Without a scope the plan is the origin only, with a `propagate-scope` DECISION hazard whose fixes name the wider scopes — a tactic stops there BEFORE writing. Each sibling is re-rendered from its OWN text (`clones.family_member_text`; a template change takes the edited text as donor only when an identity render proves the member's surface equals the origin's, and the member's own NAME is kept) and verified by reparse against its PREDICTED values; a member that cannot be is `not-propagated` residue. REACH on our own tree: 12 of 50 sibling members (value path), 4 of 50 (template path); the rest are template shapes the renderer cannot express (arity mismatch, repetition/context holes) — CART-1155 |\n| Make a TACTIC FROM AN EXAMPLE — show one before -> after, apply it wherever it matches; SHARE it with every session | Learning is itself a tactic: `tools/toolbelt.lua run learn-from-example <project> name=<n> before=@<file> after=@<file> apply=1` (MCP: `toolbelt_run { name = 'learn-from-example', params, apply }`; in a tactic: `T.use('learn-from-example', {...})`, then `T.use(<n>, { scope })`). It CREATES `<project>/.cartograph/tactics/<n>.lua` through the journal, only after the rendered entry passes its own examples in a SEPARATE process; the demonstration is its first example; a re-run is empty; it never overwrites. SHARE: `run promote-tactic <cartograph-repo> name=<n> from=<project> confirm=yes apply=1` copies it into the built-in toolbelt — ALWAYS a `promote` decision until confirmed. The toolbelt spans both roots (a project tactic named like a built-in one is refused unless it is the identical, promoted copy). The rule alone: `require('cartograph.byexample').learn(before, after)` / `.rewrite` / `.plan`; tactic verb `rewrite-by-example`. Edit REGION generalized (a bare token lifted to its node; carried-over named subterms -> holes; constants fixed); without `scope` it STOPS as a decision. An example is a CLAIM (preserves none); one that shows its rule is too general fails its own demonstration and is not written. MEASURED on lua/cartograph for `x == nil -> not x`: 492 sites in 106 files (function-level transplant: 1 of 111). Trivia-sensitive first cut (CART-1158) |\n| What does this session see, and which mount owns an address? Hold, compare or unshare that view (a namespace) | `local ns = require 'cartograph.namespace'` — VALUES, never edited in place: `ns.mount(ns0, point, target, { union = 'before'\\|'after' }?)` (a plain mount at a used point HIDES it until `ns.umount(ns, point, target?)`; a union joins it), `ns.resolve(ns, address)` → `{ point, rest, layers }` (the LONGEST point containing the address, by path segment, whatever the mount order; a miss is `nil, why, 'frontier'`), `ns.layers(ns, point)`, `ns.entries(ns)`. Unshare = keep the old value. Band ownership is this: `session.ns` mounts each band at its root, `session.owning(file)` resolves it. The toolbelt's roots are this: `toolbelt.namespace(d, root)` = the built-in dir at `tactics` + the project's `.cartograph/tactics/` unioned after; a project tactic named like a built-in one is a DECISION unless byte-identical (promoted) or OVERRIDDEN by the user's own choice — `setup{ scoped = { [root] = { tactic_overrides = { [name] = { use = 'sha256:<project file>', over = 'sha256:<built-in>' } } } } }`, both texts pinned, either changing voids it; the refusal prints the entry for the current pair, and the choice never lives in the analysed tree. CART-1160 step 4 |\n| Link one band's unresolved calls to ANOTHER band's exports — a profile mounted as its own band, or a band served by another cartograph over MCP (federation) | `local F = require 'cartograph.federation'` — `F.profile_band(runtime)` (a read-only band of the profile's declared surface); extract the project with `ts.extract(root, { profile = rt, profile_mint = false })` so its disposed calls stay PORTS; mount both (`namespace.mount(ns, root, g, { share = { rt } })` — SHARING is a mount