g the target (the builder's own when its evidence has `target = root`, else a generic `target-write`). The write needs the target MOUNTED WRITABLE: `txn.apply(store, plan, { ns })` where `txn.writable(ns, root)` finds an entry with `opts.rw`; without it the stage refuses as `decision`. In a tactic the grant is the ACCEPTED decision (`txn.grant(ns, plan, accepted)` mounts the target rw; `tactic.run(..., { ns })` supplies a base namespace). Containment is checked against the target root, the journal entry lives in the TARGET world's journal (`entry.root`; `tactic.rollback` undoes it there), the graph is not refreshed, and a dry run does not chain a cross-world step (the rest is underivable). Promotion uses it: `promote-tactic` with `into` outside the graph writes that world, `promote` being the one question. CART-1160 step 5 |\n| Answer a tactic's question ONCE and have later runs use the answer (a remembered decision) — for this exact question, or every question of one kind about files in a directory | A stopped run's `options[i].key` is the question's identity (kind + verb + evidence + touched files + target, hashed; the prose is not in it). `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/decisions.lua remember kind=<k> key=<key> [why=…]` (exact) · `remember kind=<k> dir=<dir>` / `file=<f>` (a VIEW: every `<k>` decision whose touched files ALL lie inside it; views are namespace mounts, the most specific leads and every covering view is cited) · `list` · `forget <id>`. In Lua: `require('cartograph.decisions').remember/forget/list/lookup`. `tactic.step` takes a covered decision as accepted — a remembered `target-write` is a grant — and the residue says who answered (`remembered`, `decision_id`); `tactic.run(..., { remembered = false })` asks everything again. The record is the USER's (the state dir); never written by a run, never inside the tree, and NO MCP verb writes it (toolbelt_run shows the keys). CART-1160 step 6 |\n| Let a TEAMMATE answer a tactic's question from their own checkout (a signed approval that travels; who may answer which kind) | A stopped run's options carry `portable_key` — the question named by each world's DERIVED identity (git origin + prefix), so it is the same from any checkout of that origin (a world with no origin says `portable_why` instead). The teammate signs it: `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/approvals.lua sign <private key> <principal> <kind> <portable key> <out dir> [why]` (ssh-keygen, namespace `cartograph-decision` — a git signature never replays as an approval); `list <dir>` shows each token VERIFIED or why not; `roster` = where YOUR ssh `allowed_signers` lives (state dir; never read from the tree — no roster, no signed answer). Run with the store: `tools/toolbelt.lua run … approvals=<dir>` / `tactic.run(store, term, { approvals = dir | {tokens} })`. A token answers only when its signature verifies AND config `deciders = { [kind] = { 'alice@team', 'role:reviewers' } }` (+ `roles`), looked up per file with `config.at`, routes that kind for EVERY subject to the signer; every token found that did not answer is named in the option's `refused_approvals`. The residue says `approved by <principal>`; the journal entry records `by = 'signed'`, the principal and the token id (so the ledger carries it). In Lua `require 'cartograph.approvals'` (`portable_key`, `world_id`, `sign`, `verify`, `authorized`, `load`, `write`). Signing has no MCP verb. CART-1183 |\n| Which setting applies HERE, and which entry decided it? (scoped configuration by subject — a dir, a file, a query) | `require('cartograph.config').at(subject_abs_path, key)` → `value, { source = 'scoped', scope }` / `{ source = 'global' }` / `nil, { source = 'ambiguous', entries }`. Scopes (user-side `setup{ scoped = … }`): map form `['/abs/dir' or '/abs/file'] = { key = v }`, list form `{ dir = … | file = … | query = fn(path) -> bool, name, values = { … } }`. The most specific containing scope wins (a subset beats its superset); a QUERY's containment is EXT