d, an `overlay` transport layer serving the edited texts first, spliced on the copy (edges INTO an edited file from unchanged callers re-derived). Query it with `store.scoped(over, fn)`; edits STACK (an edit on an overlay keeps the lower texts). The base graph and the disk are untouched (world_spec fingerprints the whole base); planners read the overlay through `txn.read_file`, and `store.content` / `store.stale` / the frontier-text read go through the graph's transport too (an overlay's lines, readfile's semantics); a dry `tactic.run` / `compose.run` chains these worlds step by step; `txn.apply` REFUSES a virtual graph. Acceptance: the overlay equals a rebuild of the edited world, row by row. Transport kind: `{ kind = 'overlay', files = { [abs] = text } }`. CART-1160 step 3 |\n| Run something against a SCRATCH graph without losing the one I have loaded (an example, a fixture, a probe) | `require('cartograph.store').scoped(extraction, fn)` — fn runs with the scratch graph as the lens; the caller's graph (data, generation, range columns) comes back afterwards, raise or not; nothing is re-extracted. Toolbelt examples always run this way. Anything graph-derived held OUTSIDE the store's fields registers in `store.LENS_HOOKS` (fn(data) after every restore) — `at`'s coordinate columns are the first (CART-1159). The arc: CART-1160 (mounts for graphs) |\n| Is there a named tactic for this? Re-measure a recorded discovery — does the finding still hold? | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua list` (every entry, with its examples as usage) · `run <name> <dir> [key=value ...]` (a discovery re-measures the tree; a write tactic PREVIEWS, `apply=1` writes) · `examples [name]` (run them: usage AND test). In process: `require('cartograph.toolbelt').run(store, name, params, opts)`. MCP: `toolbelt_list`, `toolbelt_run { name, params, apply? }` (a write PREVIEWS unless `apply` on a writable host — the whole tactic, `preview` = a diff per file, `worlds`). COMPOSE by name inside a tactic: `T.use(name, params)` — a write entry runs its term, a discovery is a PREMISE gate (fails ill-posed when its claim does not hold); a cycle of uses refuses. Params are coerced by the entry's declaration everywhere (`toolbelt.coerce`): a ref may be `file::name` (did-you-mean on a miss), a string `@path`, a list `a,b`; an undeclared or missing param refuses by name. Entries are plain Lua files in `lua/cartograph/tactics/` — no central list; kinds `write` (build(params) -> a tactic term) and `discovery` (measure + claim, `measures` = the ticket it tracks); each carries `examples` the fence runs (tests/toolbelt_spec.lua). The catalogue is `list` — derived from the directory (a hand list here had drifted to 8 of 15 entries). An example may declare `requires = function () -> ok, why end` (a compiler, a parser): unmet, it SKIPS and the fence counts it as a skip, never a pass |\n| Does this spec actually CATCH a regression in this guard? (a mutation check — never sed the working tree) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run mutation-check - file=<rel> before='<expr or chunk>' after='<mutated>' spec=<name>_spec [repo=<dir>] [keep=1]` — copies the repo (all but .git) to a scratch root, runs the spec there FIRST (a red baseline or a run of nothing REFUSES), applies the mutation by example (rewrite-by-example: staged, parse-guarded; a mutation that matches nothing REFUSES by name), runs the spec again: claim holds = CAUGHT, fails = SURVIVED (both summaries in the why). An expression is read as `return <expr>` when both sides are expressions (a leading `#` would otherwise read as a shebang, CART-1177). The rule generalizes carried operands — the claim says how many sites it mutated. `ground=1` applies EXACTLY the written text at its one site through the edit verb's classification (no rule is learned; a result that already occurs elsewhere refuses as drifted — widen the anchor): use it for any mutation inside a boolean chain or a comparison, where a learned rule mutates many unrelated sites and a SURVIVED describes a different mutant (MEASURED: an `or`-chain edit in xlang.lua learned as 4 rules at 10 sites SURVIVED; ground, it was CAUGHT). The working tree is never written. `spec-fails` (discovery) is the oracle alone: `run spec-fails - spec=<x>_spec [root=…]`. A mutant that makes the spec HANG (an infinite loop) is a FAILURE named `TIMED OUT` — the runner is its own process group and is killed whole at the timeout (before 2026-09-30 the tactic raised, and each hung `node` child spun on at 100% CPU). CART-1174 |\n| How much of these commits did CARTOGRAPH write (vs hand edits)? (the in-the-loop ratio) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run provenance-audit - [range=HEAD~20..HEAD] [repo=<dir>]` — every journal whose recorded root lies in the repo (journal.survey), each entry's ADDED lines matched as a multiset against each commit's `git show -U0` added lines (line level: a mixed commit splits honestly); per commit explained vs hand bytes, the overall ratio (reported, never a gate). Baseline 2026-09-28: 0.13% of the last 30 commits. Not yet: stale decisions / placeholders (the ledger, CART-1180). CART-1181 |\n| Which journal entries (and which DECISIONS) wrote this commit? Does the provenance travel with the repo? (the ledger) | Every journal entry records `decided_by` (`tactic` / `direct`) and `decisions = { { kind, key, by = accept-list \\| remembered, decision_id } }` (CART-1179). The LEDGER: one git note per commit on `refs/notes/cartograph` — `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/provledger.lua write [<rev>]` / `show [<rev>]`; `.githooks/post-commit` writes it after every commit (never fails a commit). In Lua: `require('cartograph.provenance').attribute(repo, range)` (the shared line-level attribution), `.ledger_row`, `.write_note`, `.read_note`. Notes travel only when pushed (`git push origin refs/notes/cartograph`). ★ The ledger only TIGHTENS: a note claiming a decision accepted answers nothing (pinned