cord `{ verb, args }` since CART-1191 leaf 1) and re-runs them here in order through the tactic runner; each step classifies ITSELF by its verb's goal check: `done` (already here — the same change on both sides), `applied`, `conflict` (the verb refused it STALE: this world moved at the site — a decision, keep-mine / take-theirs), `frontier` (it did not travel: a sensitive step's args are a hash, its paths name another world, or the entry recorded no invocation). PAST A STOP a later step still runs when it shares NO touched file with any stopped step (the invocation records the plan's touched set; measured: a conflict left 92.3% of later steps independent); a step that shares one — or whose files are unknown (an older entry, a sensitive or cross-world one) — is `not reached` and blocks its own files in turn; `stop_at_first` restores stopping at the first stop. The note carries a REPLAY list: every applied step of the commit's window that touched a file it changed, superseded ones included (a step whose lines a later step overwrote explains nothing, yet the later one was planned against it). Disjoint work commutes (A+replay(B) = B+replay(A), pinned). A preview plans each step against the world AS IT IS; apply=1 is the merge (journaled, undoable). In Lua `require('cartograph.replay')` — `from_notes(repo, range)`, `run(store, items, opts)`, `localize(args, root)`. ⚠ An entry the attribution credits to several commits is replayed once (CART-1195). CART-1191 |\n| How DEPENDENT are replayed steps on each other — would a conflict block work that does not depend on it? | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/replaydeps.lua <repo> <range>` — replays the range's invocations into a scratch clone of its base, once in full and once per step LEFT OUT (each step as its own replay, so a stop is not read as a dependence); step j depends on i when j's outcome changes without i. Reports the dependent-pair rate, the share of later steps a conflict at i would leave INDEPENDENT, and the agreement of two derived proxies (same `file` arg; ANCHOR — j's `before` holds a line i's `after` added). MEASURED 2026-09-28 on 2a7991d..964881d (15 edit steps): 6 of 105 pairs dependent (5.7%); a conflict leaves 92.3% of later steps independent; both proxies missed 0, same-file 14 false+, anchor 4. ⚠ SLOW: 1960 s for 15 steps (a full extract per run, and steps before the left-out one are re-run needlessly). CART-1191 leaf 3 |\n\n\n\n| Is the LOSSLESS reader safe on this language / this tree? (may learned rules and term edits be written back into it) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/identity.lua <dir> <lang> [<lang> …]` — every file of the language (by `treesitter.parse_lang`), `cst_print(read(src)) == src` byte for byte; identical / REFUSED by reason (an error node: tree-sitter could not parse it) / PRINT MISMATCH (a real reader bug — must be 0). In Lua `require('cartograph.algebraread').identity(dir, lang)`, `.read(src, lang)`, `.escapes(lang)` (a grammar type that collides with an algebra kind, e.g. `pair` in js/ts/python/json/ruby, gets a DERIVED escaped kind `pair_`; printing is unaffected). Audited 2026-09-28, zero mismatches: javascript, typescript, python, ruby, java, go (100% identical on ghost / django-oscar / discourse / big-app.spring / amass); bash 92%, c 21%, cpp 74% (the rest refused, never mis-read). rewrite-by-example takes `lang` (files chosen by parse_lang) |\n| Edit a file (or create one) at ONE site, idempotently, through the journal — instead of sed / a python replace | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run edit - file=<rel> before=@<file> after=@<file> [apply=1]` (`-` = the current directory's world, no graph extracted; a dry run PREVIEWS). In a tactic: `T.step('edit', { file, before, after })`; in Lua `require('cartograph.edit').plan(store, { file, before, after })` / `.classify(text, before, after)`. `before` must occur EXACTLY ONCE (add context); `''` creates. IDEMPOTENT by classifying the current text against BOTH images: pendi