dger note carries each entry's invocation with paths named `@<world id>/…`, or — when the entry touched a sensitive file — only `{ verb, args = reference, sensitive_files = n }`; `write_note` refuses a note `leaks` flags (class `decision`, nothing written). CART-1192 / CART-1193 |\n| Merge ANOTHER checkout's work into this one — without a line or syntax merge (their intents, replayed here) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/replay.lua <into dir> <from repo> <range> [apply=1] [approvals=<dir>]`, or OVER A SHARED REMOTE `tools/replay.lua <into dir> --remote <name> <range>` (the teammate pushed their branch AND `refs/notes/cartograph`; `replay.fetch` brings the notes into a MIRROR, `refs/notes/cartograph-remotes/<name>`, never merged into this repo's own; no git merge happens — their intents are replayed in your working tree) — reads the INVOCATIONS the ledger notes of `<range>` carry (journal entries record `{ verb, args }` since CART-1191 leaf 1) and re-runs them here in order through the tactic runner; each step classifies ITSELF by its verb's goal check: `done` (already here — the same change on both sides), `applied`, `conflict` (the verb refused it STALE: this world moved at the site — a decision, keep-mine / take-theirs), `frontier` (it did not travel: a sensitive step's args are a hash, its paths name another world, or the entry recorded no invocation). PAST A STOP a later step still runs when it shares NO touched file with any stopped step (the invocation records the plan's touched set; measured: a conflict left 92.3% of later steps independent); a step that shares one — or whose files are unknown (an older entry, a sensitive or cross-world one) — is `not reached` and blocks its own files in turn; `stop_at_first` restores stopping at the first stop. The note carries a REPLAY list: every applied step of the commit's window that touched a file it changed, superseded ones included (a step whose lines a later step overwrote explains nothing, yet the later one was