+ dependency + OTP source) | `tools/xmppserver.lua --sends [--rows] [--otp <OTP src root>]` (default: `~/git/otp_src_<installed version>`) (ejabberd: 124 of 184 sites name one record, was 80; 22 more one of several; all on the wire) |\n| What does the server REPLY to each client request — the handler run with the client's own request term (one of several replies when its arms differ), and how many of the reply's unknowns the request fills | `tools/xmppmerge.lua --responses [--rows] [--otp DIR]`; library `xmppserver.responses(merged, dir, {E, otp})` (CART-1135) |\n| Does the spec-driven ENCODER (record -> XML, `xmppspec.encode`) agree with xmpp's own generated encoder, entry by entry? | `tools/xmppencode.lua [--rows] [--only NAME]` (evaluates each entry's `encode_<name>` from source; CART-1138) |\n| The XMPP WIRE as an algebra grammar (bytes <-> #xmlel <-> record) — do its laws hold, and does it agree with the running fxml byte for byte? | `tools/xmppgrammar.lua [--rows]`; library `xmppgrammar.new{spec, otp}` / `.register(G)` (A.grammar 'xmpp': an embed prints and parses through it), `.read`, `.print_el` (CART-1138) |\n| What does a NIF return (jid's splitter, stringprep) — can a JID the tree reads be known, and do the models agree with the running runtime? | `tools/p1natives.lua [--len N] [--rows]` (exhaustive over the bytes that matter); library `p1natives.models`, handed to `erlterms.program{natives}` (on by default in `xmppserver.program`) — consulted only where the source is absent or a nif_error stub (CART-1138) |\n| Generate a CLIENT of a server from its implementation (every request its handlers accept, every reply they can give) — and check it: contract, standalone, in-process imitation, coverage of the real client | `tools/peergen.lua [--out FILE] [--rows] [--merge] [--twice]` (XMPP IQ), `tools/peergen.lua --grpc ROOT [--client DIR]` (gRPC from .proto: vendored copies merged, drift kept); core `peergen.generate(model)` (protocol-free), adapters `xmpppeer.mo