ic-for check ($forprep), `return ...` (all the varargs), %.14g exponent threshold + exact ties to even + -0, the compare message, LuaJIT hex floats; probing its gaps: a zero for-step counts UP (the sign bit). Lua-rejected programs the emitter translates: CART-1209. CART-1206 |\n| Derive a GRAMMAR from a corpus and enumerate EVERY tree up to a size (any tree-sitter language) | `require('cartograph.grammargen')`: `derive({ {name, text} }, lang)` reads each file with the lossless reader and records every node's SKELETON (tokens + slots; a one-text leaf is a zero-slot production), widening REPEATED runs (a slot list whose length varies admits every kind seen in it) and ALTERNATIVES (productions one token apart share slots); `fragment(G, { kinds = set, atoms = { kind -> texts } })` -> `count(kind, n)` (memoized, before enumerating), `each(kind, n, fn)`, `dead()` (selected kinds with no production — refuse); `print(tokens)` joins by spaces (an atom's `#` is its occurrence number). No grammar.json needed: tree-sitter ships tables, not productions. CART-1206 |\n| TRANSLITERATE a closed region of C to JavaScript (instead of re-authoring it) — e.g. LuaJIT's Lua-pattern matcher | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/cjs.lua lstrmatch <luajit src dir> <out.js>` — `cartograph.cjs` over `gcc -E` output (the HOST COMPILER expands every macro), roots = the functions to carry (reachability over the file's call graph), templates = the C-API calls at the seam (one line each). Emscripten's memory model: one byte heap, a pointer is an offset, the static arrays the code reads allocated into it; deref sign/width from the DECLARED pointee type; C's 0/1 relational results; the two structured goto shapes (backward = a labeled loop, into-`default` = a hoisted body behind a flag); anything else refused by name. The generated `lua/cartograph/luajs/lstrmatch.js` names its LuaJIT commit. MEASURED 2026-09-29: 15 functions + 2 tables + 148 LuaJIT messages, 0 refusals; every real pattern of this repo (